    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a deck build in Vancouver and need to double check my math on the load requirements. It's a cedar deck attached to a single-story house. Can you explain the difference between live load and dead load for me and how I should be approaching the calculations for post spacing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! Dead load refers to the static weight of the structure itself, including the materials (like the deck boards, beams, and framing) and structures attached to it (like posts and joists). Live load, on the other hand, accounts for the weight of people, furniture, snow, landscaping, and other changes over time, minus any wind or seismic loads. For post spacing, you'll need to consider both load types to ensure the structure can support them safely. You can use the deck size and planned occupancy to determine the live load per square foot, then add a conservative factor for the dead load. Remember to adhere to local building codes, which will specify maximum allowable loads and appropriate spacing.",
